import std.prelude.*;
// Microbench: loop string-building. Without the O(n) string-accumulator
// promotion (StrAccBegin/Append/Materialize) each `s = s + x` reallocates the
// whole string -> O(n^2) time and ~2.4 GB of throwaway allocation for 50k
// concats. With promotion it is amortised O(1) per append (single StringBuilder
// per loop, materialised once on exit): ~1 MB/run, ~50x faster.
//
// Two loops to guard both lowering paths:
//   1. literal-chunk append  (RHS is a constant — pure StrAccAppend)
//   2. iter-coercion append  (RHS is the typed iter — StrAccAppendI reads the
//      Int64 iter slot directly, eliding the per-iter BoxI/AssignBinding
//      publish + its NumberValue allocation; was ~8.5 MB, now ~1.4 MB)

var s = "";
for i in 0..50000 {
    s = s + "x";
}

var t = "";
for i in 0..50000 {
    t = t + i;
}

print(len(s) + len(t));
